+    A glyph cut out of joined writing keeps half the incoming ligature. That
+    is correct when the letter is actually joined onto something, but at the
+    start of a word there is nothing to join from and the orphan tail gets
+    drawn anyway -- it reads as an extra letter ("the" -> "tthe", "fox" ->
+    "rfox"). Only a SHORT leading stroke lying to the left of the rest of
+    the glyph qualifies, which is what separates an incoming connector from
+    a crossbar (a crossbar runs across the letter's body, not beside it)."""
